Mini-task

 Limit the dataframe to only the 2024 respondents without missing data for sex, hrs1, and childs. How many men and women are left?




gss_all |&gt; 
  filter(year == 2024) |&gt; 
  drop_na(sex, hrs1, childs) |&gt;
  count(sex)


# A tibble: 2 × 2
  sex            n
  &lt;dbl+lbl&gt;  &lt;int&gt;
1 1 [male]     859
2 2 [female]   888



ASK: Why are the numbers different than the table before?

Mini-task

 Use dplyr grammar to find the frequency, mean, and median for the variable: hrs1.




gss_all |&gt;  
  drop_na(hrs1) |&gt;
   summarize(
    freq = n(),
    med = median(hrs1),
    avg = mean(hrs1)
    ) 


# A tibble: 1 × 3
   freq   med   avg
  &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1 43328    40  41.1

Mini-task

 Compare the average and median work hours (hrs) for U.S. men and women.




gss_all |&gt;  
  drop_na(sex, hrs1) |&gt; 
  group_by(sex) |&gt; 
  summarize(
    avg = mean(hrs1),
    med = median(hrs1)
    ) 


# A tibble: 2 × 3
  sex          avg   med
  &lt;dbl+lbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
1 1 [male]    44.3    40
2 2 [female]  37.8    40
